--expect
10:7 10:3,4 02: 00: 10: 10:9 | 65 65 3 28 10 24 28 0 64
--expect-why
DERIVED FROM THE FORMAT. Each field is `<ok><bytes-left>:<ports minted>`, so a
walk that stopped early is visible as leftover bytes rather than only as a
verdict -- a scan that returned true having read half the message would pass a
check that looked at `ok` alone.

  bind      `10:7`   {:op :bind :port 7}. Reads to the end (0 left) and mints
                     port 7 -- the message `Host::caller` actually sends, and
                     the reason this walk exists: the port must exist before
                     `Host::call` delivers on it.
  two       `10:3,4` [port 3, port 4]. TWO ports from one message, in order,
                     which is what a vector of capabilities looks like.
  trunc     `02:`    `K_INT` with three bytes where eight are due. Refused,
                     nothing minted, and TWO BYTES LEFT -- the tag is consumed
                     before its payload is checked, so the cursor sits after it
                     when the refusal happens. That `2` was derived as `0`
                     first and the three targets said otherwise together, which
                     is the case where an expected answer is worth more than a
                     cross-target comparison: three agreeing implementations
                     cannot tell you that your reasoning was wrong.
  unknown   `00:`    Tag 99. There is no length to skip by, so the walk cannot
                     go on and must not pretend to. Nothing is left because the
                     tag was the whole message.
  qualified `10:`    `:ns/a`. The NAMESPACE IS PRESENT here, where every other
                     keyword in these cases has `ffffffff` for absent. Both are
                     read to the end and mint nothing, which is the pair that
                     says `NO_NS` is not being treated as a length -- a walk
                     that did would try to skip 4 294 967 295 bytes and refuse
                     the message.
  table     `10:9`   One `:int` column and one row whose cell is port 9. The
                     ROW COUNT sits after values, so a walk that read the
                     table's parts in the wrong order lands on the wrong byte
                     and either refuses or mints nothing -- and a port inside a
                     table is exactly the case that would otherwise be missed.

AND AFTER THE `|`, `wire-control-op` ON HAND-BUILT MESSAGES, each byte array
built from the documented wire format (little-endian; `K_MAP` 10 is a u32
entry count then key,value pairs; `K_KEYWORD` 6 is a u32 ns-len, or
`ffffffff` for none, then a u32 name-len and the name's bytes; `K_PORT` 15 is
a u32 id; `K_STRING` 5 is a u32 len and the bytes; `K_VECTOR` 8 is a u32 count
and the values; `K_INT` 3 is eight bytes). Every expected number is
`op + 8 * (port-id + 1)` from the doc on `wire-control-op` itself, derived
before running anything -- `port-id + 1` is 0 when the message has no port,
and `op` is 0 for anything `wire-control-op` does not recognise as `:bind`,
`:unbind`, `:close` or `:snapshot`.

  bind            `65`  `{:op :bind :port 7}`: op 1, port 7 -> `1 + 8*8 = 65`.
  bind (reorder)  `65`  THE SAME MESSAGE with `:port` written before `:op` in
                        the map's entries. Same answer, because a map's `:op`
                        and `:port` are found by NAME, not by position --
                        getting this wrong would make the parser's output
                        depend on an encoder's key order, which nothing in
                        the format promises.
  close           `3`   `{:op :close}`: op 3, no port -> `3 + 8*0 = 3`. The
                        ONLY case here with no `:port` key at all, which is
                        what pins `port-id + 1 = 0` as the no-port answer
                        rather than some other sentinel.
  snapshot        `28`  `{:op :snapshot :port 2}`: op 4, port 2 ->
                        `4 + 8*3 = 28`.
  unbind          `10`  `{:op :unbind :port 0}`: op 2, port 0 (the id itself
                        is zero, not absent) -> `2 + 8*1 = 10`. Without the
                        `+1` a present port 0 would read identically to no
                        port at all, which is exactly the off-by-one the
                        encoding exists to avoid.
  qualified op    `24`  `{:op :x/snapshot :port 2}`: `:op`'s value carries a
                        NAMESPACE, so `wire-plain-kw-len` -- which only
                        matches a keyword with NO namespace -- answers -1 for
                        it, `op` stays 0 (unrecognised), and the port is
                        still read: `0 + 8*3 = 24`. The pair with `string-op`
                        below is what shows `:port` is read independently of
                        whether `:op` was understood.
  snapshot+extra  `28`  `{:op :snapshot :port 2 :extra [1 2]}`: the SAME
                        answer as plain `snapshot`, because an unrecognised
                        key's value is skipped by `wire-scan-at` (here a
                        vector of two ints) rather than changing the result
                        -- `wire-control-op` reads `:op` and `:port` and
                        nothing else, exactly as the flint code it replaces
                        read `(:op m)` and `(:port m)` off a map and ignored
                        the rest.
  vector          `0`   `[1]`: not a map at all, so the tag check at the top
                        of `wire-control-op` takes the "not K_MAP" branch,
                        skips the whole value with `wire-scan-tagged`, and
                        answers the "nothing understood" code -- `op` 0, no
                        port, `0 + 8*0 = 0`.
  string op       `64`  `{:op "bind" :port 7}`: `:op`'s value is a STRING, so
                        `wire-plain-kw-len` -- which only matches a KEYWORD --
                        refuses it the same way the qualified keyword above
                        was refused; `op` stays 0 and the port is still read:
                        `0 + 8*8 = 64`. Paired with the qualified-op case to
                        show the same guard firing for two different reasons
                        a value can fail to be "a plain keyword": the wrong
                        TAG here, a namespace there.
